From 953813abdfc657d4de3f020549730a9f12ac17f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 21:27:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] link-status: what the kernel asks where it marks
 a link Bad

The connector property "link-status" is the kernel's: its default value
is Good, and where something fails during or after a modeset (i915 after
a DisplayPort link training that failed) the driver sets it to Bad and
sends a hot-plug event. The display then gets no pixels. The kernel's
documentation (drm_connector.c, "link-status") asks the program that
drives the display to perform a new modeset with link-status set to
Good, which has the link trained again; a commit that leaves it Bad may
still leave the display without pixels, and user space can only set it
to Good (a request for Bad is a no-op). Kodi never did either.

LinkStatus.h holds the rule, without libdrm, so that a host test runs it
against a model of the kernel:

- ValuesOf(), LinkOf(): Good and Bad as the enum of the property names
  them; a connector without it does nothing.
- Changes(): the commit that has the link trained again is link-status
  Good on the connector and nothing else, with a modeset allowed. The
  kernel duplicates the state of every object of the commit and of the
  modeset that the change of link-status makes (connectors_changed), so
  the mode, HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA, the Colorspace, the depth, the Dolby
  Vision transport and its InfoFrame, the 3D layout, VRR and the
  framebuffer go out as the kernel has them; no mode is chosen.
- CRetrain: the link is read only after a hot-plug event, once after a
  change of mode of Kodi's, and when a look it asked for is due; one
  commit, then two more at 2 s and 8 s where the link reads Bad again,
  then it stops until Kodi's next change of mode; nothing while the
  output is off or not Kodi's, while a change of mode of Kodi's is
  pending (that commit sets it Good itself: CarriesGood()), or where the
  connector cannot be read.
- LookAt(): one look, through OUTPUT_STATE::IKms.

The reason OutputState.h gives for leaving link-status out of the
capture of native output said that asking for Bad retrains the link;
it is said as the kernel does it now.
